Catalysis
Organic pollutants are stable chemical substances occurring widely in aquatic environments. Their long-term impact on the entire ecosystem has directly resulted in irreversible damage worldwide. Thus, advanced tech­ nology is required to restore the water quality. The photo-Fenton process has been widely applied due to its high removal rate of organic pollutants in water. Fe-based metal-organic frameworks (Fe-MOFs) not only have good porosity and catalytic properties but also are non-toxic, abundant, and inexpensive and have promising appli­ cations in practical engineering. Fe-MOFs are good photocatalysts and can couple well with Fenton reagents compared with other MOFs. This paper reviews the research progress of Fe-MOFs in the photo-Fenton process and proposes a method to overcome the limitations of the photo-Fenton process. The different factors influencing these processes, the stability and reusability of the materials after use, and the mechanism of the photo-Fenton reaction after the modification of different materials are discussed. Finally, conclusions and the future prospects of Fe-MOFs in photo-Fenton systems for the treatment of wastewater containing organic pollutants are proposed. This study provides a valuable resource for researchers using Fe-MOFs to remove organic pollutants from water bodies.
## 1. Introduction
Water pollution caused by economic growth, industrialization, and rapid urbanization has become one of the most serious threats to mankind [1-4]. Over the last few decades, numerous synthetic organic chemicals, such as antibiotics, pesticides, dyes, and flame retardants, have been widely used, which lead to water pollution [5-7]. Antibiotics are used not only for pain relief and sterilization in daily life but also for disease control in aquaculture and animal husbandry [8]. Dyes are used for dyeing and medical disinfection, among others. These chemicals are essential to modern people’s lives. However, owing to the high stability of some organic pollutants under sunlight and high resistance to biodegradation [9], conventional wastewater treatment plants cannot remove them effectively [10]. Their overuse and delayed degradation leads to the inevitable discharge of their residues into the aquatic environment [11]. According to surveys, both surface water and groundwater in China are contaminated by organic pollutants at various degrees [12], which ultimately enter the human body via the food chain or drinking water [13]. Therefore, it is necessary to develop effective water treatment methods to remove these organic pollutants.
Numerous methods have been investigated for the treatment of organic pollutant residues in water, including physical removal [14], biological treatment [15] and chemical degradation [16]. Physical methods, such as adsorption, sedimentation, flocculation, and filtration, can only separate organic pollutant residues from water but cannot effectively degrade them [17], and most organic pollutants in the environment can be removed by biological treatment [18]. However, the artificial introduction of active organisms into the aquatic environ­ ment may disrupt the ecological balance between their biomes and cause irreversible damage to the ecosystem. Therefore, chemical degradation has received significant attention. Advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) are one of the most promising technologies for solving organic pollutants [19]. Currently, research results based on the in situ generation of reactive oxidation radicals, such as hydroxyl radical ( OH), sulfate radical $(\bullet{\cal S}0_{4}^{~-})$ , and superoxide radical $(\bullet0_{2}^{-})$ , for the removal of organic pollutants are widely applied. Among the various AOPs, the application of Fenton reagents has gained much attention owing to their mild reaction conditions and simple operation. However, they have some disadvantages in practical applications, such as a narrow pH range, non-renewable catalysts, and deposition of iron-containing sludge [20,21]. To avoid these disadvantages, researchers combined them with photocatalytic reactions to form photo-Fenton systems. Photocatalysis using solar radiation as an energy source has a high redox capacity and low cost; therefore, it has broad prospects in the field of environmental remediation [22]. However, the low transfer efficiency of photocarriers and the rapid complexation of photogenerated electronhole pairs are two evident drawbacks of photocatalysis, which limit the widespread application of photocatalysts in practical wastewater remediation. The addition of electron acceptors (e.g., $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and per­ sulfate (PS)) is an effective method to ameliorate these drawbacks. Photocatalysis and Fenton reactions can also produce synergistic effects by not only suppressing the recombination of photogenic electron-hole pairs, but also by reducing the energy consumption during the activa­ tion of Fenton reagent [23]. The non-homogeneous photo-Fenton reac­ tion has attracted widespread attention because of its high ability to generate reactive radicals, a wide range of reaction $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}.$ , and the recy­ clability of the catalyst.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline porous materials with a periodic network structure formed by the self-assembly of metal ions or metal clusters with organic ligands, with ultra-high and tunable pore sizes and large specific surface areas. MOFs have strong potential for antibacterial applications [24], gas storage [25], adsorption and separation [26], sensing [27] and photocatalysis [28]. For example, photosensitive MOFs can be used for the rapid control of wound in­ fections [29]. Among the various reported MOFs, Fe-MOFs have wide applications in catalysis. The abundance, environmental friendliness, excellent magnetic selectivity, and cost-effectiveness of Fe-based nonhomogeneous catalysts improve their suitability for a photo-Fenton process [30,31]. Notably, Fe-MOFs have a strong absorption of visible light due to the presence of numerous Fe-O clusters, which are prone to the generation of holes and photogenerated electrons. When Fe-MOFs are activated by light, electrons and holes are generated in the con­ duction band (CB) and valence band (VB), respectively. These photo­ generated electrons have a strong reducing power and can reduce the structure of Fe(III) to Fe(II) in situ on the surface of Fe-MOFs. When $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is added, the reaction of Fe(II) with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ produces a large amount of •OH, which can degrade organic pollutants. Since the discovery of the MIL-101 framework (MIL $\c=$ Materiaux de l ’institut Lavoisier) by Ferey et al. [32] in 2005, Fe-MOFs have shown significant photocatalytic potential. According to the study by Wang et al. [33], the photo­ degradation of tetracycline (TC) by MIL-101(Fe) reached $96.6\%$ when the initial TC concentration was $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ .
To date, several reviews have reported the activation of Fenton re­ agents, such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or PS, by MOFs. For example, Huang et al. [31] reviewed the application of various MOFs in $\bullet{\mathsf{S O}}_{4}^{-}$ based advanced oxidation processes (SR-AOPs), and Joseph et al. [34] reviewed the synthesis methods of Fe-MOFs and their application in different organic wastewater; however, none of these extensively explored the factors that influence the reaction process. In this study, we combined Fe-MOFs with Fenton reagents and reviewed the recent research advancements and operating conditions of Fe-MOFs in Fenton reagents under light condi­ tions. At the same time, a method to overcome the limitations of photoFenton application was proposed, and the mechanisms of different FeMOFs modification methods under photo-Fenton were systematically described. This review mainly includes: 1) a summarized analysis of the research progress of Fe-MOFs as photo-Fenton catalysts, 2) a systematic description of the influence of various factors on the photodegradation of organic pollutants, 3) a systematic analysis of the degradation mechanism of Fe-MOFs under photo-Fenton conditions, 4) the problems existing in the research of Fe-MOF-based materials in recent years and
future development prospects.
## 2. Application of Fe-MOFs in photo-Fenton system
To investigate the actual removal of organic pollutants by Fe-MOFs under photo-Fenton conditions, many studies have been conducted. Catalysts range from single materials to various modifications derived to address the shortcomings of a single material (Fig. 1). Moreover, Fenton reagents range from the addition of hydrogen peroxide to PS with stronger oxidation potentials.
## 2.1. Pristine Fe-MOFs
To date, several Fe-MOFs have been developed using various types of organic ligands [30]. For example, the combination of octahedral chains of benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}B D C)}$ and $\mathrm{FeO}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ leads to the flexible structure of MIL-53(Fe) [35]. The MIL-88 series can be prepared from $[\mathrm{Fe_{3}(\mu_{3}-O)(C O O)_{6}}]$ clusters and linear dicarboxylic acid ligands of different lengths [36], and the combination of $[\mathrm{Fe_{3}(\mu_{3}-O)(C O O)_{6}}]$ sec­ ondary building units (SBUs) and $\mathrm{\mathrm{H_{2}B D C}}$ linkers results in MIL-101(Fe) [37]. Viswanathan et al. [38] prepared a rod, rhombus, and spindleshaped MIL-88A(Fe) by controlling the temperature, reaction time, and solvent in the hydrothermal method. Compared with the diamond and spindle shaped MIL-88A(Fe) particles, those with rod-shaped par­ ticles have a smaller size and lower electron-hole complexation rate, thus improving the photocatalytic efficiency. However, Fe(II) in the material is continuously consumed by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , resulting in a slower re­ action rate. Wu et al. [39] used a MIL-101(Fe)/photo $\mathrm{{\cdotH}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{2}$ system for the degradation of tetracycline hydrochloride (TC-HCl) and demon­ strated that visible light can accelerate the Fe(II)/Fe(III) cycle in the photo-Fenton system based on the Fe-O clusters in the frame (Fig. 2a). This is also the reason for the efficient degradation of TC-HCl Similarity, Xing et.al [40] prepared a two-dimensional metal–organic framework of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ by adding 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaaminotriphenyl hexahydro­ chloride and concentrated ammonia to a $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ solution, which had $\mathrm{FeN}_{4}$ active sites and superior conductivity, thereby accelerating the Fe(III)/ $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ cycle. The acceleration of the $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ cycle in the system may become a research hotspot.
Sulfate radicals can be generated by activating peroxydisulfate (PDS) [42] or peroxymonosulfate (PMS) [43]. However, these deactivated substances limit the oxidative capacity of pollutant decontamination, and the use of the transition metal ion Fe(II) has been found particularly beneficial for the activation of PDS and PMS [44]. In recent years, photoFenton technology based on sulfate radicals has received increasing interest. For example, Zhang et al. [45] prepared MIL-88A (Fe) using a hydrothermal method, which achieved $99.8\%$ removal of TC-HCL in 80 min under visible light and PS. Similarly, Yin et al. [41] used a MIL-100 (Fe)/photo-assisted PDS activation (PPA) process for sulfamethoxazole (SMX) degradation and proposed the $\mathrm{Fe(II)/Fe(III)}$ cycle in the PPA process (Fig. 2b). Photogenerated electrons are transferred to the MIL100 surface and react with Fe(III) to form Fe(II), which then signifi­ cantly improves the activation efficiency of PDS, resulting in the gen­ eration of •OH and $\bullet s{0_{4}}^{-}$ radicals for the removal of SMX. Table 1 shows the pristine Fe-MOFs used for the degradation of organic pollut­ ants in the photo-Fenton system.
Nevertheless, the application of pristine Fe-MOFs in the photoFenton system has some drawbacks. First, the poor stability of MOFs in water bodies limits their application [54]. Second, the photocatalytic activities of pristine Fe-MOFs remain relatively low because of the rapid charge recombination and poor charge separation [28]. Therefore, many researchers have proposed the synthesis of MOF composites to improve their optical properties. When MOFs are combined with other materials, new interfaces or heterojunctions are formed, which strongly influence the optical Fenton catalytic performance. Hence, it is crucial to develop interfaces of MOF composites that can effectively couple the catalytic process of photogenerated electrons and the generation of
Fenton radicals [55]. In addition, the synthesis of new pristine Fe-MOFs is another approach. Although there are many types of pristine Fe-MOFs, few are currently used in photocatalysis. For photocatalysts with pristine MOFs, future trend is to meet the response range of visible light and solve the problem of photogenerated electron recombination.
## 2.2. Metal doping
Bimetallic systems with immobilized metal ions, metal nanoparticles (NPs), and inorganic nodes or SBUs of MOFs can enhance the catalytic, electronic, stability, and luminescent properties of MOFs [56,57]. Nguyen et al. [58] synthesized an Al/Fe metal-organic framework (Al/ Fe-MOF) by which the degradation efficiency of rhodamine B (RhB) reached $96\%$ . According to ultraviolet-visible diffuse reflectance spec­ trum, the introduction of ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ into Fe-MOFs can significantly enhance the absorption of Al/Fe-MOF in the visible region, thus improving the photocatalytic activity.
The energy consumption during the activation of Fenton reagent can be further reduced by incorporating metals into Fe-MOFs Farrokhi et al. [59] synthesized phosphonate-based STA-12(Fe, Mn) bimetallic organic framework materials to remove dyes. The Mn (II) of STA-12(Fe, Mn) can accelerate the decay of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to generate •OH through a Fenton-like process. Similarly, Li et al. [60] prepared Fe-Co Prussian blue analogs, which are a type of metal-organic framework with a simple cubic lattice structure formed by bridging the octahedral $[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]^{3-}$ anion group with iron ions; Co is chemically similar to Fe and also reacts with Fenton reagent to produce numerous free radicals. Although this accelerates the decomposition rate of contaminants, the material not only leaches iron ions but also cobalt ions and contaminates the water. The leaching concentrations of the doped metals in the metal-doped Fe-MOFs are listed in Table 2.
Metal NPs are widely used owing to their unique physical and chemical properties and excellent catalytic activity. However, because of their high mobility, metal NPs tend to aggregate during sample syn­ thesis or oxidation reactions [69]. Therefore, researchers are currently focused on the loading of metal NPs on Fe-MOF carriers to provide NPs with good dispersibility. Liang et al. [70] prepared a $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}100(\mathrm{Fe})$ nanocomposite by alcohol reduction and achieved theophylline degra­ dation with $99.5\%$ efficiency, which was over $30\%$ higher than that of the original MIL-100(Fe). The introduction of Pd NPs reduced the in­ tensity of photoluminescence spectroscopy (PL); therefore, the photo­ generated carriers in Pd@MIL-100(Fe) have a longer lifetime than those in MIL-100(Fe). Fe-MOFs are non-toxic and harmless; however, when they are loaded with NPs, the toxicity concentration must be maintained within a controlled range to avoid the contamination of water by the photocatalyst itself. Table 2 shows the metal-doped Fe-MOFs used for the degradation of organic pollutants in the photo-Fenton system.
## 2.3. Construction of heterojunction
Matching the electron-electron band structure in heterojunction photocatalysts facilitates the separation of photogenerated carriers and improves photocatalytic activity [71]; therefore, combining these pris­ tine Fe-MOFs with other organic-compound, inorganic non-metal, and metal oxide semiconductors to form heterojunctions can effectively inhibit photogenerated electron-hole recombination, resulting in higher photocatalytic efficiency.
Polyaniline (PANI) is a good organic compound semiconductor with π-π conjugation, which can promote interfacial electron migration and accelerate photogenerated electron-hole separation. In addition, PANI can improve light utilization in the visible region $(>420\ \mathrm{nm})$ and enhance photocatalytic activity. For example, Chen et al. [72] con­ structed PANI/MIL-88A (Fe) to improve the separation of photo­ generated carrier and increase the conductivity using a one-pot hydrothermal method. The degradation of bisphenol A (BPA) by this compound reached almost $100\%$ after $40\mathrm{min}$ under the PANI/MIL-88A $\mathrm{(Fe)/vis/H_{2}O_{2}}$ system. Similarly, An et al. [73] t constructed MIL-100 (Fe)/PANI II-scheme composites and achieved the degradation of thiamphenicol with a higher efficiency than that by MIL-100(Fe) under visible light irradiation. This is due to the faster interfacial charge transfer and the lower photogenerated electron-hole complexation rate of the MIL-100(Fe)/PANI composite than that of pure MIL-100(Fe).
Among various inorganic non-metal semiconductor photocatalysts, graphitic carbon nitride $(g{\cdot}{\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathsf{N}}_{4})$ exhibits a unique photovoltaic struc­ ture and physicochemical stability [74]. It can be easily loaded onto the surface of Fe-MOFs to form heterojunction photocatalysts, which can considerably improve the photocatalytic performance. Gong et al. [75] added $2\%$ $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ to MIL-101(Fe) to form a heterojunction compound, which exhibited good light absorption, charge carrier separation, and excellent photocatalytic degradation of BPA in the presence of PS. The transfer of photogenerated electrons can also accelerate the decompo­ sition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Li et al. [76] constructed a $\mathrm{g{\mathrm{-}C_{3}N_{4}/N H_{2}\mathrm{-}}}$ MIL-88B(Fe) heterojunction and based on the results of the electron spin resonance (ESR) technique reported that photoelectron transfer from the CB of $g\cdot$ $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ to $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) effectively leads to more free electrons in the CB of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) and enhances the Fenton-like activation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . However, under a low $g{\mathrm{-C}}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ load, the material may not achieve the expected degradation efficiency, whereas under a high load, a “covering effect” occurs. In particular, excess $g{\mathrm{-C}}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ may (i) lead to a reduction of the active sites on the surface of Fe-MOFs and (ii) shield FeMOFs from light absorption and photogenerated electron production [76].
Metal oxides can be used as primary catalysts or combined with other catalysts to construct heterojunctions to form multi-component catalysts that possess significant catalytic potential. Ahmad et al. [77] prepared stable MIL-100(Fe) using MOF-5 as a template and then successfully synthesized mesoporous MIL-100(Fe) loaded with $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanospheres using an in situ self-assembly method. Its mesoporous structure could improve the diffusion and accessibility of molecules within the MOF channel and enhance the catalytic activity. In addition, these metal oxides provide metal ions to enhance the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in solution. He et al. [78] synthesized $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot100(\mathrm{Fe})$ in which $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ provides photogenerated electrons to react with Fe(III) on MIL-100(Fe) to form Fe(II), thereby supplying ferrous ions to react with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ also acts as a catalyst, which helps $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot100(\mathrm{Fe})$ to achieve better levofloxacin removal than MIL-100(Fe). When $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is wrapped with Fe-MOFs to form a shell, the thickness of the shell also affects the degradation rate. The thicker the shell, the more Fe-MOF is available to produce more photogenerated electrons and OH, but it becomes more difficult for photogenerated electrons to enter the core region. The photogenerated electrons of the MOF shell layer can only be used effectively if the outer layer is adequately thick and the entire shell layer is not too thick [79].
Owing to the synergistic effects of multiple combinations of different materials and methods, researchers have also investigated ternary composites by combining heterojunctions with metal covalency, among other methods. Li et al. [80] first synthesized $g{\mathrm{-C}}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}/$ pyromellitic dii­ mide (PDI) and effectively adjusted its energy band structure by adjusting the electrons in PDI to match the energy band positions of $g\cdot$ - $\mathrm{C_{3}N_{4}/P D I}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Fe), while the matching of the electronic energy band structure in the heterojunction photocatalyst facilitated the separation of light-generating carriers, thus improving the photo­ catalytic activity. Zhong et al. [61] first introduced copper ions into MIL (Fe), resulting in an asymmetric coordination in Fe-O-Cu clusters of MIL $\mathrm{(Fe/Cu)}$ , which enlarged the pore size and improved the transmission efficiency. $\mathtt{C u_{2}O}$ was then transformed into a heterojunction using a Cu bridge. Although $\mathtt{C u_{2}O}$ can effectively absorb visible light and generate high photocurrents, it produces a rapid combination of electrons and holes, whereas MIL(Fe/Cu) has a high ability to accept photogenerated electrons. Moreover, Cu in MIL(Fe/Cu) can significantly enhance the interfacial connection between $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O}$ and MOFs, thus reducing the re­ action pathway between photo-induced electrons and Fenton generated radicals. The construction of heterojunctions is a common and easy modification method for Fe-MOFs to improve degradation efficiency, mainly by reducing the recombination of electrons and holes. Table 3 shows the heterojunctions constructed with Fe-MOFs for the degrada­ tion of organic pollutants in the photo-Fenton system.
## 2.4. Defect engineering
Defect engineering is an important tool for modulating the physical/ chemical properties of material surfaces to enhance catalytic reactions. Crystal defects reduce the coordination around the atoms and expose more active sites, which can accelerate photocatalytic reactions. How­ ever, an increase in the number of lattice defects in the crystal acceler­ ates the combination of photogenerated carriers, which can have a negative impact on photocatalysis. Wang et al. [101] modified $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL88B(Fe) using three different ligands (benzoic acid, pyrrole, and pyrrole2-carboxylic acid) as monodentate ligands. Several monodentate ligands and ligand vacancies are introduced in MOFs to improve photocatalytic light absorption and electron-hole separation and also increase Fe Lewis acid sites to promote the activation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Oxygen vacancy (OV) defect modulation is a common approach that can significantly affect the electronic structure of the oxide surface and thus alter its chemical reactivity. Integrating the dual active sites of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and OVs to fully expose the active sites results in a higher photo-Fenton effect. Xu et al. [102] synthesized $\scriptstyle\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{-Fe_{2}O_{3-x}}$ materials with controllable OVs using benzimidazole (BIm)-modified Fe-MOFs as templates by the sol­ vothermal method, and density functional theory showed that both $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and OVs promote the dissociation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to generate •OH for the degradation of methylene blue (MB).
Most of the metals in Fe-MOFs are bound by organic linkers, and the strong coordination of metal ions with organic ligands significantly re­ duces the exposed active sites of most Fe-MOFs, which reduces the Lewis acid sites and prevents them from activating $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Establishing coor­ dination unsaturated metal centers (CUSs) to form defective sites as Lewis acid centers may increase photo-Fenton activity [103]. Fe-MOFs act as Lewis acid catalysts in the presence of CUSs. In contrast, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is present in the formation of Lewis bases during Fenton-like reactions. CUSs have a strong affinity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , generating a large amount of •OH to remove organic pollutants [104]. Therefore, the introduction of CUSs in Fe-MOFs increases the activation efficiency of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and thus im­ proves the photocatalytic degradation effect.
Currently, mixed-valence MOFs are usually prepared by thermal activation methods, such as heating on MIL-100(Fe) under vacuum or in an inert atmosphere, which can produce Fe(II) CUSs [105]. However, the resulting mixed-valence Fe-MOFs are unstable and must be reac­ tivated for recycling. Another method is to replace a fraction of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ with $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}$ ⋅ $4\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ in the preparation process [106]. Guo et al. [107] prepared MIL-100(Fe) with mixed-valence CUSs, which can improve charge-migration capacity and reduce the complex iron chains of electrons and holes in the presence of mixed-valence Fe(II)/Fe(III), simultaneously using visible light to accelerate the Fe(II)/Fe(III) cycle, and thus enhancing the photocatalytic activity. The ratio of $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ to Fe (III) was 0.063 for CUS-MIL-250 and 0 for MIL-101(Fe). The introduc­ tion of Fe(II) CUS improves the decomposition efficiency of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . In addition, because the electron-rich linker provides electrons to the metal center, CUSs can be reduced after removing the ligand, further pro­ moting the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to OH. The formation of hetero­ junctions of mixed-valence MOFs with other substances results in a synergistic effects. Yang et al. [108] synthesized $\mathrm{Ag_{3}P O_{4}}/$ mixed-valence MIL-88A(Fe) and adjusted the Fe(II)/Fe(III) ratio to modulate the elec­ tronic structure of MIL-88A(Fe), resulting in higher photocatalytic per­ formance. Compared with the band gap of single-component photocatalysts, that of $\mathrm{Ag_{3}P O_{4}},$ /mixed-valence MIL-88A(Fe) is signifi­ cantly reduced, which facilitates higher visible light absorption and faster electron hopping. Incorporating metals into MIL with mixed va­ lences produces improved synergistic effects. Wu et al. [109] synthe­ sized low-crystalline bimetallic L-MIL-53 (Fe, Mn) with several unsaturated active sites using a one-pot hydrothermal method at low temperatures. The low-crystalline MOFs have more active sites and de­ fects than the high-crystalline MOFs, and the synergy between Fe and Mn nodes is enhanced in the low-crystalline state. Simultaneously, visible light contributes significantly to the Fenton process by providing photogenerated electrons to participate in the Fe(II)/Fe(III) and $\mathbf{Mn(II)}/$ $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathrm{III})$ cycles. The reaction rate in Eq. (1) is several orders of magnitude higher than that of Eq. (2), leading to excessive accumulation of ferric ions (Fe(III)), Fe(III) may react with OH to form $\mathrm{Fe(OH)}_{3}$ , thus it is essential to accelerate the $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ cycle. The construction of mixed-valence Fe-MOFs is an important strategy for enhancing the Fe (III)/Fe(II) redox cycle; therefore, replacing a part of Fe(III) with Fe(II) is a novel and promising approach. Table 4 shows the Fe-MOF defect en­ gineering used for the degradation of organic pollutants in the photoFenton system.
