## 2.5. Composite with graphene and its derivatives
Graphene oxide (GO) is a functionalized graphene with various oxygen-containing groups, such as hydroxyl, carboxyl, carbonyl, and epoxy groups. The introduction of oxygen-containing groups not only makes graphene chemically stable, but also provides surface modifica­ tion active sites and large specific surface area for the synthesis of GObased materials. The∙refore, when combined with Fe-MOFs, they can provide a larger specific surface area to effectively disperse the attached materials and preven∙t agglomeration as well as provide more catalytic centers and increase the number of active sites for reaction with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Due to its superior carrier mobility and high conductivity [111], GO improves the separation of photoexcited electron-hole pairs, and elec­ trons in GO can be trapped by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Thus, many researchers have loaded Fe-MOFs on GO to improve photocatalytic degradation efficiency.
Lin et al. [112] prepared GO@MIL-101(Fe) by loading MIL-101(Fe) onto $15\%$ GO, which expanded the visible light absorption from $520\mathrm{nm}$ to $570\mathrm{nm}$ compared with MIL-101(Fe) in a light/ $\mathrm{{MOF/H_{2}O_{2}}}$ system; the removal rate of Tris(2-chloroethyl) phosphate (TCEP) reached $95\%$ within $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . Liu et al. [113] synthesized MIL-88A(Fe)/GO nano­ composites and achieved $100\%$ removal of RhB within $80~\mathrm{min}$ using $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ at a concentration of $20~\mathrm{mM}$ . This is because the introduction of
GO increases the visible light absorption intensity and also narrows the band gap energy. Notably, Xie et al. [114] prepared oxide/MIL-88A(Fe) membranes by using a vacuum filtration method and embedded M88A into GO. The membrane material could remove dye wastewater using the photo-Fenton reaction and significantly improved the separation efficiency and flux of the membrane; the separation effect could reach $97.87\%$ even after 12 cycles. Fakhri et al. [85] prepared $\operatorname{AFG@MIL-101}$ (Fe) ternary composites with amine-functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , GO, and MIL101(Fe) to magnetize them, while accelerating the conversion of Fe(II) and $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ by electron transfer between GO layers for diazinon and atrazine pesticides with excellent photocatalytic degradation effects. These studies have demonstrated that because GO is a strong electron acceptor, it can be used to effectively separate charge carriers and pre­ vent their recombination, thus improving the photocatalytic activity. In addition, previous research has shown that the light absorption capacity of Fe-MOFs is enhanced by the incorporation of GO. Thus, the MOFs/GO hybrids can be ideal catalysts for photo-Fenton systems. Table 5 shows the Fe-MOF composites with graphene and its derivatives used for the degradation of organic pollutants in the photo-Fenton system.
## 2.6. Amine-functional Fe-MOFs
Functionalized MOFs are one of the currently applied modification methods. It is well known that the synthesis of MOFs requires the addition of organic linkers, and the photocatalytic properties and pho­ tostability of the materials can be improved by replacing the organic linkers with appropriate amino groups. Moreover, the amino linkers have a strong electron donor ability, which reduces the band gap energy of the materials. Zhang et al. [119] synthesized MI $\mathrm{L}{\cdot}101{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ by replacing terephthalic acid with 2-aminoterephthalic acid and concluded that the amino group might absorb visible light and transfer electrons to the Fe-O clusters of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2};$ therefore, MIL- ${\cdot}101{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ removed amaranth (AMR) by the activation of oxo ketone to generate •OH and $\bullet s{0_{4}}^{-}$ radicals under visible light. Xie et al. [120] prepared PVDF/ $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) membranes using the phase-inversion method. The incorporation of NM88B highly improved the hydrophilicity of the membranes and facilitated its permeation and antifouling properties as well as the extensive visible light absorption of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe), achieving a degradation rate of $97\%$ for MB in 70 min. Shi et al. [121] prepared $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) using a microwave-assisted solvothermal method. Under visible light irradiation, the amine-functionalized organic linkers in the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) structure and the Fe-O clusters were excited, while the photogenerated electrons in the organic linkers were transferred to the Fe-O clusters. Therefore, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) has a higher photocatalytic activity than MIL-88B(Fe), and their lightharvesting efficiency can be flexibly adjusted by simply modifying the organic linkers in the MOF structure. Table 6 shows the aminefunctional Fe-MOFs used for the degradation of organic pollutants in the photo-Fenton system.
## 3. Factors affecting performance of Fe-MOFs in photo-Fenton
External environmental factors have a significant impact on the removal rates of organic pollutants. The influencing factors were grouped into the following six categories described below in detail, based on the variables to be controlled in the experiment. An accurate understanding of the optimum reaction conditions is beneficial for the efficient utilization of Fe-MOFs and the practical application of the photo-Fenton process.
## 3.1. pH
The pH of the reaction medium has a significant effect on the surface charge, stability of the catalyst, present form of active material, and redox potential. In most reaction systems, the degradation of contami­ nants by the catalyst is the highest under acidic conditions because the redox potential of •OH is higher and $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ is prone to leaching under acidic conditions, causing homogeneous Fenton reactions [95]. Zhang et al. [45] studied the effect of initial pH on TC degradation by MIL-88A and reported that the optimal $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\pH}}$ was 3, which verified that iron ions could be leached under acidic conditions. Acidic conditions also promote the activation of PS.
However, under highly acidic conditions, proton-promoted dissolu­ tion leads to partial skeletal destruction, resulting in a significant loss of the exoskeletal iron catalyst. Mohammadifard et al. [46] found that the removal of formaldehyde by the photo-Fenton reaction of MIL-100(Fe) in acidic media was lower than that at $\mathrm{pH}~6.6$ . This was due to the CUSs coordinating with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and releasing $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ , thus acidifying the so­ lution and reducing the solution $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}.$ . In this process, the carboxylic acid linker is protonated and the photocatalyst undergoes structural degradation. Therefore, the stability of the photocatalyst is improved, and the efficiency of the photocatalyst increases when the initial $\mathsf{p H}$ of the solution increases from 3 to 6.6. Similarly, Wu et al. [66] investi­ gated the optimal pH for the photo-Fenton reaction of $\mathrm{FeNi_{1/60}}$ -BDC on MB/methyl orange (MO) and found that compared to other initial pH values, a high concentration of Fe ions was dissolved in the solution at $\mathrm{pH}~1.0$ , thus demonstrating that Fe-MOFs are extremely unstable in strongly acidic media.
In alkaline solutions $\left(\mathrm{pH}\right.=11\$ ), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is unstable and readily de­ composes into $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Eq. (3)), and Fe-MOFs readily form inactive iron-based ions and precipitates oxyhydroxides (Eq. (4)), which de­ creases the rate of $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Fe(II)}$ transition [48,83,123]. In addition, when the pH value gradually increases, more $\mathrm{{OH}^{-}}$ occupy the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ sites, thus effectively inhibiting the interaction between $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and resulting in a lower degradation rate. Strong alkaline solutions also destroy the Fe-MOFs structure. Liu et al. [113] analyzed the X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) patterns of M88/GO-9 composites before and after the photocatalytic experiments at $\mathsf{p H}11$ , and the results suggested that M88/GO-9 may be partially decomposed.
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{2H_{2}O_{2}{\rightarrow}O_{2}+2H_{2}O}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{F e^{2+}+H_{2}O_{2}{\rightarrow}F e O^{2+}+H_{2}O}}\end{array}
$$
In addition, $\mathsf{p H}$ also affects the potential of the material surface; when the pH exceeds the isoelectric point, the material becomes nega­ tively charged. For example, Mei et al. [52] measured that the isoelectric point of MIL-53(Fe) was at $\mathrm{pH}=5.3$ , and the surface of MIL-53(Fe) is positively charged under strongly acidic conditions, and the strong hydrogen bond between the $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ bond and the O-O bond in PMS under acidic conditions inhibits the interaction between PMS and the positively charged MIL-53(Fe) surface. pH also affects the form of con­ taminants. When $\mathrm{pH}<3.3$ , TC exists in protonated form $\mathrm{(TCH_{3}^{+})}$ , and when $\mathrm{pH}>7.7$ , it occurs in the monoanionic form $(\mathrm{TC}^{-})$ [83]. Similarly, Gao et al. [35] found that the adsorption effect of MIL-53(Fe) on pol­ lutants becomes poor at alkaline pH, possibly because clofibric acid (CA) mostly transforms into anions in alkaline solutions, and the surface of MIL-53(Fe) gradually becomes negatively charged, resulting in repul­ sive electrostatic interactions between MIL-53(Fe) and CA anions.
Notably, Ren et al. [124] explored the relationship between pH and the activity of three pristine Fe-MOFs: MIL-53(Fe), MIL-100(Fe), and MIL-101(Fe). MILs showed high catalytic activity at pH 3, and decreased under weakly acidic conditions. However, under neutral conditions, MILs catalytic activity was very undesirable. Many modification methods are available for improving the suitability of Fe-MOFs under neutral conditions. For example, Zhang et al. [119] showed little dif­ ference in the degradation of AMR at a pH of 3–9. The $\mathsf{C u_{2}O/M I L}$ (Fe/ Cu) prepared by Zhong et al. [61] could completely degrade thiacloprid within $25~\mathrm{{min}}$ at an initial pH of 7.47. However, these studies did not extensively investigate the mechanism by which the modification method affected or enhanced the applicability of pH.
## 3.2. Fenton reagent and catalyst
The concentrations of the catalyst and Fenton reagent are important parameters that affect the reaction rate. Within a certain range, the higher the catalyst concentration, the higher is the number of active sites of the catalyst, the higher the number of free radicals, and the higher the catalytic efficiency. Liu et al. [123] reported that the removal of TC-HCl increased from $89\%$ to $97\%$ when the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/M I L}{\cdot}125$ dosing concen­ tration was increased from $0.05~\mathrm{g/L}$ to $0.1\ {\ g}/\mathrm{L}$ . A suitable amount of catalyst can provide numerous active sites, which can form reactive oxygen species. However, the degradation rate decreased slightly when the catalyst was injected at a concentration of $0.2\ {\ g}/{\mathrm{L}}$ . This is because when the amount of catalyst is too high, two conditions will occur. First, a large amount of catalyst in solution can increase the turbidity of the suspension and cause light scattering, thus reducing the light trans­ mission and effective light absorption of the system. Second, the competition reaction caused by a large amount of catalyst can reduce the number of effective radicals (Eqs. (5 and 6)) [33,45].
