## 3.4. Temperature
The solution temperature also affects the efficiency of the reaction. High temperatures can enhance the transfer of reactants and electrons, thus accelerating the surface activation and reaction efficiency of FeMOFs. Hu et.al [50] investigated the effect of temperature on the removal of tris(2-chloroethyl) phosphate by MIL-101(Fe)-activated PS and found that $308\mathrm{K}$ was the most effective at 288 K, 298 K, and $308\mathrm{K}$ However, as the temperature exceeded a certain limit, the degradation rate decreased. This is because the Fenton reaction is a typical exothermic reaction, and increasing the temperature hinders the pro­ duction of OH [118]. MacDonald et al. [88] investigated the effects of temperature on the removal of total organic carbon (TOC) by Fe/MIL47. The removal of TOC decreased with increasing temperature because of the increased rate of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decomposition at high tempera­ tures, thereby limiting the availability of OH required for the complete removal of organic compounds from $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . Controlling the optimum re­ action temperature at room temperature and increasing the applicable range of temperature can improve the limitation of its practical application.
## 3.5. Light conditions
## 3.6. External electric field
An external electric field is added to the photo-Fenton reaction sys­ tem, and Fe-MOFs undergo a reduction reaction at the cathode to reduce $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Eq. (15)), which is then activated by Fe-MOFs to produce OH for the degradation of organic pollutants. The added Fe-MOF can be used as an active site to enhance the oxygen reduction reaction activity, thus increasing the yield of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Wang et al. [92] studied the perfor­ mance of (Fe/Co) MOFs/CNF in a photo-electro-Fenton system and found that the degradation efficiency of perfluorooctanic acid was $20\%$ higher than that of the photo-Fenton process. Ye et al. [49] tested the removal of bezafibrate by Fe-based 2D MOFs with and without an applied electric field and obtained similar conclusions. The potential of the electric field is also considerably important; an elevated potential increases the electron transfer and thus accelerates the rate of hydrogen peroxide production. However, if the potential is exceedingly high, the single-electron or four-electron reduction of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generation reaction inhibits the two-electron reduction of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Therefore, adding a suitable electric field to the photo-Fenton system not only improves the photodegradation efficiency but also avoids the transport and storage of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to reduce the cost [130], thus attracting the attention of many scholars. Although adding an electric field can improve the degradation efficiency, energy consumption is also signif­ icantly increased, which must be considered in future research.
