## 4. Stability and reusability
The stability and reusability of these materials are important con­ cerns for practical applications. Improved stability of materials is essential to prevent hydrolysis and increased reusability is required to reduce running costs and secondary contamination.
## 4.1. Stability
MOFs are highly susceptible to skeleton collapse due to hydrolysis in the presence of water and gas and weak metal coordination bonds. After the reaction, metal ions are partially leached from the catalyst, which leads to the loss of active sites and reduces the photocatalytic effect [131]. In Fe-MOF systems, the surface morphology and crystalline structure of MOFs after the reaction, metal leaching, and reusability are mainly analyzed. Zan et al. [132] tested $\mathrm{Fe_{2}O_{3}}$ photocatalytic materials synthesized by MIL-53(Fe) and found that the amount of leached Fe ions was $<0.7\mathrm{mg/L}$ in each of the six cycles, which met the safety standard. To improve the stability of the Fe-MOFs, Huang et al. [90] synthesized $\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\mathrm{A}@6\%$ PDI to encapsulate and protect MIL-88A using PDI. The amount of leached Fe ions of MIL-88A@PDI $(0.02{-}0.03\ \mathrm{mg/L})$ was lower than that of MIL-88A ( $2.10{-}3.34\ \mathrm{mg/L})$ . The accumulation of organic intermediates at the active sites of the photocatalyst can also lead to a reduction in the degradation efficiency. In addition, numerous studies have been conducted to understand the structural changes in the material before and after the reaction. Zhang et al. [45] confirmed that there was no significant change in the morphology and structure of MIL88A via scanning electron microscopy, Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), and XRD analyses on MIL-88A before and after five reactions, which proved its good stability. Yan et al. [118] examined the FTIR and XRD spectra of CNT@MIL-101(Fe) before and after the reac­ tion and found no significant chemical or structural changes. However, in practical applications, it is not sufficient to cycle the catalyst five times without changing the structure; it has to be cycled 20 times or more. The optimum reaction conditions must also be determined to maintain the activity and stability of the catalyst after many cycles.
## 4.2. Reusability
Currently, catalysts are mostly used in powdered form, which poses a major problem for water safety, as they cannot be fully recycled. In addition, if the particulate catalysts are not removed from the water column, secondary contamination may occur. To address these issues, many researchers have prepared magnetic catalysts. $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is a common superparamagnetic nanoparticle that is easily recovered from reaction mixtures with magnets (Fig. 3a); therefore, it is often combined with MOFs to magnetize them [133]. For instance, Liu et al. [96] synthesized $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L}{\cdot}53(\mathrm{Fe})$ nanocomposites with saturation magnetization strength of 52.6 emu ${\bf g}^{-1}$ compared with $2.5\times10^{-4}$ emu ${\bf g}^{-1}$ of MIL-53 (Fe). Wang et al. [83] prepared a $\mathrm{ZIF{-}8/M n F e_{2}O_{4}}$ magnetic catalyst by modifying $\mathrm{MnFe_{2}O_{4}}$ with ZIF-8, and its saturation magnetization value was $43.24\mathrm{\emu\g^{-1}}$ , indicating that the material could be separated outside the bottle with a magnet. However, the reusability of the ma­ terial must be considered. Rasheed et al. [97] cycled MIL-100(Fe) $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{CA}$ seven times, and the removal rate of TC remained above $80\%$ , which proved its good recyclability.
In addition to improving the magnetic property of the material, the integration of MOFs onto a suitable supporting matrix can improve re­ covery [136]. Nanocellulose with abundant carboxyl and hydroxyl groups has many advantages, including being lightweight, cost-efficient, and non-toxic [137]. Over the past decades, nanocellulose has been developed as a scaffolding matrix (e.g., aerogels, foams, and mem­ branes). Composite materials constructed by the immobilization of FeMOFs into cellulosic matrices have been increasingly studied owing to their excellent performance in water treatment. Jing et al. [134] pre­ pared MIL-88B(Fe)/gelatin aerogel-150 by directly mixing MIL 88B(Fe)
with a sol-gel precursor. The catalyst was directly collected and des­ orbed in anhydrous ethanol by shocking for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Fig. 3b). Liu et al. [135] fabricated monolithic foam with a MIL-101- ${\bf\cdot N H}_{2}(\mathrm{Fe})$ loading of up to $49\%$ , wherein the MOF particles were more uniformly dispersed through the support of the nanocellulose foam, and the hydrophilic composite foams contained sufficient active sites for the photocatalysts to quickly react with pollutants after capturing visible light (Fig. 3c). Gao et al. [115] prepared PAA $@$ NM88B/GO membranes, whose MB interception rate was ${>}98\%$ for all five cycles, and the MB flux remained unchanged (Fig. 3d). Improvement in the recycling performance of catalysts can reduce material wastage and costs, which are important factors for various applications.
