## Radical $^+$ organic pollutants→CO2 + H2O
In the second category, the main degradation mechanism is the activation of the Fenton reagent, and visible light, metal active sites, and photoexcited electrons play an important role in the activation process.
In addition, some studies have shown that $^10_{2}$ also play a major role in the degradation [31]. Li et al. [60] preparation of Fe-Co Prussian blue analogues in capture experiments and found that both •OH and $\bullet{\mathsf{O}}_{2}^{-}$ were not major radicals; however, the degradation efficiency of RhB was almost completely inhibited when $^10_{2}$ was captured. The authors sug­ gested that $^{\bar{1}}{\bf O}_{2}.$ , as a direct active species for RhB degradation, might be generated by the fast radical reaction process in the structural vacancies of Fe-Co PBAs. Most of Fe-MOFs in the photo-Fenton reaction are photoassisted Fenton activation, which may be due to the good coupling effect of the Fe contained in Fe-MOFs with the Fenton reagent.
For different modification methods, the reaction mechanisms may also be different. Cu, Al, Co, Ni, and other metal ions added to the FeMOFs also react with the Fenton reagent to form additional radicals to improve the removal efficiency. Cheng et al. [68] prepared CuFeO MOFs, where photogenerated electrons turn $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ into $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ , which can react with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce hydroxyl radicals (Fig. 6a). When the het­ erojunctions of Fe-MOFs are prepared with other materials, the problem of electron-hole recombination can be considerably solved. Su et al. [122] synthesized $\mathrm{g{\mathrm{-}C_{3}N_{4}/N H_{2}}}$ -MIL-101(Fe), where photogenerated electrons migrated from the CB of $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ to the CB of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe), forming a type II heterojunction, thus achieving a rapid separation of electron-hole pairs (Fig. 6b); Wang et al. [82] synthesized ${\bf W O_{3}/M I L-}100$ (Fe), in which photogenerated electrons migrated from the CB of ${\mathsf{W O}}_{3}$ to the VB of MIL-100(Fe), thus effectively avoiding annihilation (Fig. 6c). These electrons on the surface of the material also accelerate the acti­ vation of the Fenton reagent, resulting in the generation of more free radicals. Using defects in the crystal, such as the formation of CUSs, the Fe-MOFs expose more Fe Lewis acid sites and thus react with more $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ and Fe(III) in CUS-MIL-250 prepared by Guo et al. [107] formed strong affinity complexes with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and reacted more rapidly with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Fig. 6d). Wang et al. [101] introduced pyrrole-2-car-boxylic acid into $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L{-}}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe}) $ , promoted light absorbancy and electron-hole separation capacity for photocatalysis, and strengthened Fe Lewis acidity to improve redox capacity of $\mathrm{Fe(II)/Fe(III)}$ for photocatalysis (Fig. 6e). Introduction of GO as an electron acceptor into Fe-MOFs prevent agglomeration as well as provide more catalytic centers and increase the number of active sites for reaction with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . GO encap­ sulated in uniform MIL-88A(Fe) by Liu et al. [113], GO improves the separation of photoexcited electron-hole pairs, and electrons in GO can be trapped by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Fig. 6f).
ESR is commonly used to determine the reactive substances that play a major role in the photo-Fenton reaction. In general, •OH and $\bullet{\mathrm{O}}_{2}^{-}$ are the two main reactive species, which can be detected by ESR spectros­ copy using 5,5-dimethylpyridine-N-oxide (DMPO) as a trapping reagent. Zhong et al. [61] observed a characteristic quartet peak of the DMPOOH adduct with an intensity ratio of 1:2:2:1 in the ESR signal and six characteristic peaks of DMPO- $\bullet0_{2^{-}}$ adducts, which confirmed that the main radicals in the reaction were OH and $\bullet{0_{2}}^{-}$ . In addition, in situ FTIR was used to characterize the active sites of the materials during the reaction. Recently, Wu et al. [125] used in situ FTIR to probe the CUSs of FeNi-LDH/BMNSs in the photo-Fenton reaction. These CUSs behave as Lewis acid sites and may promote and facilitate the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules (Lewis base).
## 6. Conclusions and outlooks
Fe-MOFs are new porous inorganic-organic materials with numerous iron sites, which can make them excellent Fenton catalysts. In addition, the metal nodes of MOFs can be considered as isolated semiconductor quantum dots that can be directly activated under light irradiation or by organic linkers as light-absorbing antennas, making Fe-MOFs a more suitable photocatalyst in photo-Fenton systems. The synergistic effect of Fe-MOFs and photo-Fenton accelerates the electron-hole separation of the catalyst and the activation of the Fenton reagent to achieve a high degradation rate with this combination. Their broad application pros­ pects are attributed to their lost cost and abundance. Among the factors influencing the photo-Fenton reaction, pH, Fenton reagent and catalyst, light conditions, and complex ratios are particularly important. The photo-Fenton reaction is a redox reaction in which a specific wavelength of light stimulates the production and transfer of electrons from the catalyst, generating a large amount of •OH radicals in the solution to degrade organic pollutants. In addition, the common problems of MOFs have been addressed, such as poor stability has been overcome by different modification methods and insufficient recyclability has been improved by magnetizing the material and later separating it using magnets.
Although significant progress has been made in the application of FeMOF-based materials in the photo-Fenton process, research in this area remains underdeveloped and requires further investigation. The following issues are yet to be addressed before using this technology in practical applications.
(1) Although OH produced by the reaction can oxidize organic matter, it also combines with the iron ions in solution to form Fe $\mathrm{(OH)}_{3}$ precipitates when pH is higher than 4. The resolution of these sludge by-products requires further attention.   
(2) Although the removal effect of contaminants is optimal under acidic conditions, the highest leaching rate of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ also occurs under these conditions. In bimetallic Fe-MOFs, precious metals, such as Pd and Ni, are generally used, which may increase the cost, and their leaching amounts cannot be ignored because the leaching of these metal ions leads to secondary pollution and
limits the practical application of Fe-MOFs in the removal of organic pollutants.
(3) Fe-MOFs are very effective in removing organic pollutants under photo-Fenton conditions, but they cannot fully mineralize organic matter and produce harmful intermediates. Therefore, the combination of conventional treatment methods, such as biology and chemistry (applied electric field), may be a research hotspot.   
(4) Currently, research on Fe-MOFs focuses on individual aspects with properties, such as abundant nanopores, large specific sur­ face area, and good thermal stability; however, further research is required to fully understand their advantages.   
(5) All current studies have been conducted in the laboratory and have only explored the removal of a single pollutant, whereas actual water bodies contain many different organic pollutants and must also consider the effects of water flow and water quality fluctuations. Therefore, these techniques must be studied further in the context of actual wastewater treatment.   
(6) Few scholarly studies have investigated the presence of excessive Fenton reagent in the reaction and the impact of excessive Fenton reagent on the environment.   
(7) The degradation performance of the material decreases signifi­ cantly after multiple utilization, and the maintenance of the initial degradation efficiency must be further studied.   
(8) A higher number of organic ligands can be used to improve the photocatalytic activity by developing new pristine Fe-MOFs.   
(9) Develop Fe-MOFs that can be excited by natural sunlight to reduce energy consumption.
